<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeanne d'Albret</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jeanne III of Navarre · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeanne d'Albret was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1555 to 1572 and sovereign of Béarn, the daughter of Henry II of Navarre and Marguerite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>. She converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, made it the official religion of Béarn and became a leading figure of the Huguenot cause in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry III of Navarre</a> became Henry IV of France in 1589.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret.jpg" alt="Jeanne d'Albret" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of François Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeanne-albret-navarre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jeanne was born on 16 November 1528 at Saint-Germain-en-Laye. From the age of two she was raised apart from her parents at the Château de Plessis-lèz-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, at the wish of her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>, and was taught by the humanist Nicolas Bourbon. On 13 June 1541, at the age of twelve, she was forced for political reasons to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">William, Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a>, and had to be carried to the altar by the Constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Anne de Montmorency, after protesting in writing. The marriage was annulled in 1545. On 20 October 1548 she married Antoine de Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme; of their five children only Henry and Catherine reached adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On the death of her father on 25 May 1555 Jeanne and Antoine became joint rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, and they were crowned at Pau on 18 August. Antoine's frequent absences left her to rule Béarn alone. Influenced by her mother's leanings towards religious reform, she publicly embraced the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> on Christmas Day 1560, which made her the highest-ranking Protestant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. She declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> the official religion of her kingdom, banished priests and nuns, prohibited Catholic ritual and commissioned a translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> into Basque and Béarnese. She also reformed the economic and judicial systems of her domains.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> began in 1562 Antoine sided with the Catholics, and Jeanne left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for Béarn. He died that year of wounds suffered at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. She refused an offer of marriage from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> that required her return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and she was summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by Pius IV to be examined for heresy; the threat was never carried out. When the third war broke out in 1568 she took refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> with her son, wrote manifestos, borrowed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a> on the security of her jewels and worked with Admiral de Coligny.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jeanne was the chief negotiator of the Peace of Saint-Germain-en-Laye in August 1570. Its terms led to the marriage contract between her son Henry and Marguerite, sister of Charles IX, signed on 11 April 1572 after talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> at Chenonceaux. She fell ill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 4 June 1572 and died five days later; a rumour that Catherine had poisoned her with perfumed gloves was not borne out, since an autopsy showed natural causes. She wrote poetry and memoirs justifying her leadership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>. Her son succeeded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry III of Navarre</a> and in 1589 became Henry IV of France, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> line of kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeanne d'Albret” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through <b>Jeanne d'Albret</b> it reached Henry IV, who in 1607 united his personal lands, including Foix, with the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-foix">County of Foix</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Queen Jeanne d'Albret</b> converted to Calvinism and promoted it, though most Basques stayed Catholic.</blockquote><a href="/state/lower-navarre">Lower Navarre</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Queen Jeanne d'Albret</b> made Calvinism the official religion in the 1560s.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Viscounty of Béarn</a></li><li><blockquote>It reached the Bourbons through <b>Jeanne d'Albret</b>, and when her son Henry IV became king in 1589 it was merged with the crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-limoges">Viscounty of Limoges</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Germain-en-Laye, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.9,2.09&amp;year=1556" aria-label="Saint-Germain-en-Laye on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jeanne d'Albret"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5118 5942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5118 5942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.9,2.09&amp;year=1556">What was here in 1556? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
